#heading(numbering: none, outlined: false)[Abstract]

A contended lock decides whose critical section runs next. Patel et al. showed that FIFO acquisition is not enough: threads with longer critical sections receive more lock time and subvert the CPU scheduler's allocation. Usage-fair locks address this directly. u-SCL accounts per-thread hold time, grants ownership in millisecond lock slices, and penalizes threads that exceed their share; CFL carries the virtual-runtime policy of Linux's Completely Fair Scheduler over to locks, reordering the wait queue by priority-weighted virtual lock hold time. Both restore a fair allocation in real systems. Both, however, keep the thread being served as the thread that executes the critical section, so every fair hand-off migrates the protected data to another core, and both recover locality by relaxing fairness: u-SCL lets a slice owner reacquire the lock for milliseconds, and CFL favors waiters on the current socket. Each also needs a policy for a thread that is owed service but is not waiting when the lock decides, for example because it is still waking from a futex wait: u-SCL holds the lock idle for its slice owner, while CFL hands the turn to whoever is waiting. We call these two costs _migration_ and _absence_. Delegation locks eliminate migration, because a single combiner thread executes every published request where the data already resides; absence remains, and becomes an explicit waiting policy. We present FC-PQ, a flat-combining lock whose combiner serves pending requests from a priority queue keyed by each requester's accumulated critical-section time. We evaluate it inside redb, an embedded transactional key-value store with a single-writer lock, with core frequency pinned at 3.0 GHz. With 1-record and 64-record writers, FC-PQ raises Jain's fairness index over critical-section time from 0.85--0.86 under the FIFO MCS queue lock to 0.94--0.995, sustains 1.17--1.34× MCS's transaction rate, and incurs 10--18 cache-to-cache transfers per transaction where MCS incurs 93--126. It ties u-SCL, because redb's writers resubmit immediately and are never absent. Blocking FC-PQ's waiters exposes absence directly: with uniform transactions, blocking matches u-SCL's CPU time at spinning throughput, but with mixed sizes short-transaction clients wake too late and fairness drops as low as 0.65.
